                        <div data-vport="1024" data-panel-mobile="figures" data-figures-panel-mobile="figures-panel"><div id="figures-tab-pane" class="tab-pane" role="tabpanel" aria-label="" aria-hidden="true"><div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" data-method="figures"><figure id="f0001" class="article__tabFigure openInViewer material-fig" aria-label="Flowchart of a study with 40 screened individuals, 35 randomized into PBBT+FES and PBBT groups, followed by assessments." data-type="figure"><button aria-label="Open in viewer" data-tooltip-position="bottom" class="figure__image-btn"><img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," aria-labelledby="f0001" data-src="/cms/10.1080/10749357.2026.2664217/asset/25d67ad3-edb3-4ce8-809f-681d12504f10/assets/graphic/ytsr_a_2664217_f0001_oc.jpg"></button><figcaption class="hasDescription"><div class="core-caption-heading"><div class="core-caption-label">Figure 1.</div></div><div class="core-caption-content" style="max-height: 66px; overflow: hidden;"><div role="paragraph">Participant flow diagram. Of 40 individuals screened for eligibility, 5 were excluded prior to randomization (3 did not meet inclusion criteria; 2 declined to participate). Thirty-five participants were randomly allocated to perturbation-based balance training combined with functional electrical stimulation (PBBT+FES; <i>n</i>â€‰=â€‰17) or perturbation-based balance training alone (PBBT; <i>n</i>â€‰=â€‰18). All randomized participants completed the intervention and were assessed at post-intervention and 3-month follow-up. No participants were lost to follow-up. All 35 participants were included in the intention-to-treat analysis.</div></div><button class="btn figure__read_more read-more">Read more<i class="icon material-icons">expand_more</i></button><div class="core-caption-details-action"><div class="core-caption-details"><span class="format"><span class="details-format">JPG</span>â€¢<span class="details-size">229 KB</span></span></div></div></figcaption></figure><figure id="f0002" class="article__tabFigure openInViewer material-fig" aria-label="Two images showing a balance training setup: a schematic and a laboratory photo with two people." data-type="figure"><button aria-label="Open in viewer" data-tooltip-position="bottom" class="figure__image-btn"><img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," aria-labelledby="f0002" data-src="/cms/10.1080/10749357.2026.2664217/asset/630bd7ef-7f2a-4f26-bcf7-21d84d0e21c9/assets/graphic/ytsr_a_2664217_f0002_oc.jpg"></button><figcaption class="hasDescription"><div class="core-caption-heading"><div class="core-caption-label">Figure 2.</div></div><div class="core-caption-content" style="max-height: 66px; overflow: hidden;"><div role="paragraph">Experimental setup for perturbation-based balance training. (A) Schematic representation of the custom-designed, computer-controlled movable platform used to deliver unpredictable forward slip-like perturbations during treadmill walking. The platform was capable of forward translations triggered near heel strike of the paretic limb. Participants walked along the platform while secured in an overhead safety harness that prevented falls without providing body-weight support. (B) Laboratory photograph of a participant walking on the perturbation platform during a training session, supervised by a licensed physical therapist. The black arrow indicates the direction of forward platform translation during slip perturbation delivery.</div></div><button class="btn figure__read_more read-more">Read more<i class="icon material-icons">expand_more</i></button><div class="core-caption-details-action"><div class="core-caption-details"><span class="format"><span class="details-format">JPG</span>â€¢<span class="details-size">200 KB</span></span></div></div></figcaption></figure><figure id="f0003" class="article__tabFigure openInViewer material-fig" aria-label="Three graphs showing quadriceps and gastrocnemius activation levels and ground reaction force during stance phase." data-type="figure"><button aria-label="Open in viewer" data-tooltip-position="bottom" class="figure__image-btn"><img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," aria-labelledby="f0003" data-src="/cms/10.1080/10749357.2026.2664217/asset/dd126057-405e-4a54-a2b3-c2bb5f01f1c9/assets/graphic/ytsr_a_2664217_f0003_oc.jpg"></button><figcaption class="hasDescription"><div class="core-caption-heading"><div class="core-caption-label">Figure 3.</div></div><div class="core-caption-content" style="max-height: 66px; overflow: hidden;"><div role="paragraph">Illustration of peak activation interval (PAI) calculation during the stance phase of gait. Representative surface electromyographic (EMG) activation profiles of the quadriceps (vastus lateralis, VL) and gastrocnemius (medial gastrocnemius, GM) are shown, time-normalized to 100% of the gait cycle. In healthy gait, VL reaches peak activation during early-to-mid stance (weight acceptance), while GM reaches peak activation during late stance (propulsion), producing a clear temporal separation between the two muscles. Peak activation for each muscle was defined as the maximum amplitude within the constrained stance-phase window. The PAI~VL-GM~ (shaded interval) was computed as the temporal difference, expressed as a percentage of the gait cycle, between the peak activation of VL and the subsequent peak activation of GM. A larger PAI indicates greater phase-dependent differentiation between quadriceps and gastrocnemius activation â€” a pattern associated with more selective, task-appropriate extensor coordination. Vertical ground reaction force (GRF) is shown in the lower panel to demarcate stance phase boundaries (initial contact to toe-off).</div></div><button class="btn figure__read_more read-more">Read more<i class="icon material-icons">expand_more</i></button><div class="core-caption-details-action"><div class="core-caption-details"><span class="format"><span class="details-format">JPG</span>â€¢<span class="details-size">195 KB</span></span></div></div></figcaption></figure><figure id="f0004" class="article__tabFigure openInViewer material-fig" aria-label="Line graph showing Peak Activation Interval during gait cycle for PBBT+FES and PBBT groups at Baseline, Post and Follow-up." data-type="figure"><button aria-label="Open in viewer" data-tooltip-position="bottom" class="figure__image-btn"><img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," aria-labelledby="f0004" data-src="/cms/10.1080/10749357.2026.2664217/asset/2c7b1c38-c54d-4a45-b0ea-776d33ee5dec/assets/graphic/ytsr_a_2664217_f0004_b.gif"></button><figcaption class="hasDescription"><div class="core-caption-heading"><div class="core-caption-label">Figure 4.</div></div><div class="core-caption-content" style="max-height: 66px; overflow: hidden;"><div role="paragraph">Changes in neuromuscular coordination across time points. Peak activation interval (PAI~VL-GM~) between the vastus lateralis and medial gastrocnemius during the stance phase of gait is shown for the PBBT+FES group (closed circles, solid line) and the PBBT group (open circles, dashed line) at Baseline, post-training, and 3-month follow-up. Higher PAI values indicate greater temporal separation between quadriceps and gastrocnemius peak activation, reflecting more phase-dependent intermuscular coordination. Data are presented as meanâ€‰Â±â€‰standard deviation. Within-group comparisons: both groups demonstrated significant increases in PAI from Baseline to post-training (PBBT+FES: <i>p</i>â€‰=â€‰0.038; PBBT: <i>p</i>â€‰=â€‰0.002); in the PBBT group, the improvement was also significant from Baseline to follow-up (<i>p</i>â€‰=â€‰0.012), whereas this change did not reach significance in the PBBT+FES group (<i>p</i>â€‰=â€‰0.390). Between-group comparisons: no significant Groupâ€‰Ã—â€‰Time interactions were detected at any assessment point (all <i>p</i>â€‰&gt;â€‰0.05), indicating comparable PAI trajectories across groups.â€‰*â€‰indicates a significant within-group change from Baseline (<i>p</i>â€‰&lt;â€‰0.05). nsâ€‰=â€‰not significant between groups.</div></div><button class="btn figure__read_more read-more">Read more<i class="icon material-icons">expand_more</i></button><div class="core-caption-details-action"><div class="core-caption-details"><span class="format"><span class="details-format">GIF</span>â€¢<span class="details-size">35 KB</span></span></div></div></figcaption></figure><figure id="f0005" class="article__tabFigure openInViewer material-fig" aria-label="Line graph showing CoM stability for PBBT+FES and PBBT groups at Baseline, Post training and Follow up." data-type="figure"><button aria-label="Open in viewer" data-tooltip-position="bottom" class="figure__image-btn"><img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," aria-labelledby="f0005" data-src="/cms/10.1080/10749357.2026.2664217/asset/f5b0dbe8-acc2-492d-b16d-5998a4ae89d5/assets/graphic/ytsr_a_2664217_f0005_b.gif"></button><figcaption class="hasDescription"><div class="core-caption-heading"><div class="core-caption-label">Figure 5.</div></div><div class="core-caption-content" style="max-height: 66px; overflow: hidden;"><div role="paragraph">Changes in reactive balance stability across time points. Center-of-mass (CoM) stability following unexpected forward slip perturbations is shown for the PBBT+FES group (closed circles, solid line) and the PBBT group (open circles, dashed line) at Baseline, Post-training, and 3-month Follow-up. CoM stability is expressed as the instantaneous CoM motion state, combining anteroposterior position and velocity relative to the posterior boundary of the base of support at post-slip touchdown, with higher (less negative) values indicating greater dynamic stability and lower risk of backward balance loss. Data are presented as meanâ€‰Â±â€‰standard deviation. Within-group comparisons: both groups demonstrated significant improvements in CoM stability from Baseline to Post-training (PBBT+FES: <i>p</i>â€‰&lt;â€‰0.001; PBBT: <i>p</i>â€‰=â€‰0.015); a significant improvement from Baseline to Follow-up was also observed across groups (<i>p</i>â€‰=â€‰0.009), though a modest attenuation was detected in the PBBT+FES group between Post-training and Follow-up (<i>p</i>â€‰=â€‰0.043). Between-group comparisons: no significant Groupâ€‰Ã—â€‰Time interactions were detected at Post-training (<i>p</i>â€‰=â€‰0.37) or Follow-up (<i>p</i>â€‰=â€‰0.87), indicating equivalent reactive balance trajectories across groups.â€‰*â€‰indicates a significant within-group change from Baseline (<i>p</i>â€‰&lt;â€‰0.05). nsâ€‰=â€‰not significant between groups.</div></div><button class="btn figure__read_more read-more">Read more<i class="icon material-icons">expand_more</i></button><div class="core-caption-details-action"><div class="core-caption-details"><span class="format"><span class="details-format">GIF</span>â€¢<span class="details-size">35 KB</span></span></div></div></figcaption></figure></div></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
